;;; A relation's name glued to its candidate count in `-H` — M1f **W42**, register row
;;; `W42/h-long-relation` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), filed by S1f.5.6's T1f.5.6.5
;;; (2026-11-09) beside `W42/class-label-04-open`: the same column logic on another surface.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two people and one relation between them whose name is eighteen characters,
;;; `lives-next-door-to`. `is-a` is closed by `:no-hypothesis`, so H is the two facts the blind
;;; enumerator proposes over the one relation, `(lives-next-door-to Ann Bob)` and
;;; `(lives-next-door-to Bob Ann)`, and no rule restricts them: R is the cube, four states.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `ein solve FILE -H` prints H's form after the read-out, a line per relation: its name, then
;;; how many candidates are over it. The name is padded to sixteen characters and the count
;;; printed straight after, so a name of sixteen or more touches it, and until S1f.5.6's
;;; T1f.5.6.6 the line read `lives-next-door-to2 candidates, 0 dead alone`: a reader reads a
;;; relation `lives-next-door-to2`, and the register test's own reader of `-H` lost the
;;; relation. Right: one space at least, `lives-next-door-to 2 candidates, 0 dead alone`.
(relation is-a T T
  :why "{1} is a {2}")
(relation lives-next-door-to Person Person
  :why "{1} lives next door to {2}")
(is-a Ann Person) (is-a Bob Person)
(solve :no-hypothesis (is-a))
